## Context

Author's note, 2026-08-22, verbatim:

> File rules/workflow.md is dirty after being auto-added to for a few weeks.
> - Too many detailed explanations
> - Some rules probably contradict others in IDH (e.g. the do not delegate simple tasks).
> - It ends on a cosmetic
> - Claude Code idiosyncrasies should be separated, so that the harness loader can have the common core on pi.
>
> Other files are probably suffering from the same accumulation.
>
> The periodic rules review and cleaning mechanism designed to prevent this drift needs fixing too.

The review-cadence marker (`last-reviewed:` on each rule body, warned on by
`scripts/warn-stale-rules.sh` after 30 days) records passes, not edits, so a
file that grows by one bullet per incident keeps a fresh stamp while it bloats.
`rules/README.md` § Review cadence already states this weakness.

## Mechanism, isolated 2026-09-09

The tree was never lazily loaded. Claude Code (2.1.266) walks
`~/.claude/rules/` at session start and sorts each `.md` on one question:

- frontmatter declares `paths:` → **conditional**, the body arrives only when
  the session touches a matching file;
- no `paths:` → **resident**, the body is in the system prompt of every
  session, in every project, labelled "user's private global instructions".

Evidence: the loader in the CLI bundle filters the directory walk on the
presence of frontmatter globs, `claudeMdExcludes` documents
`**/some-dir/.claude/rules/**` as an exclusion target, and the `/init` prompt
tells users that `.claude/rules/` files "are loaded automatically alongside
CLAUDE.md and can be scoped to specific file paths using `paths` frontmatter".
It matches the observation exactly: `state.md` was the one body absent from the
prompt, and it is the one file that carried `paths:`.

Consequences for this ticket:

- The pointer-table design was not under-used, it was never in force. The index
  described in full what the runtime had already shipped in full — 942 words of
  duplicate, itself resident, and cat-ed a second time by `on-start.sh`.
- `inject_rule_on_edit.py` was re-serving resident bodies (1 069 in 101 days).
  Scoping is what gives it a job: `paths:` is coarse (any `.tex` brings all
  three doctypes and both langs), the hook resolves the exact one.
- Trimming `workflow.md` and `git.md` is no longer housekeeping. They are 14 000
  of the ~21 600 resident tokens, on every conversation, in every project.

**Verify on the author's machine before trusting this** (it could not be run
from the container that isolated it): drop two files in `~/.claude/rules/`, one
with a `paths: ["*.py"]` frontmatter and one without, each carrying a distinct
canary sentence, then ask a fresh session in a non-Python directory which
canaries it can see. Expected: the unscoped one only.

## Done 2026-09-09 (branch claude/organisation-harnais-v2zvy8)

- `paths:` frontmatter on the eight path-expressible bodies (coding-python,
  coding-bash, prose/_all, doctype/×3, lang/×2): resident 28 400 → 21 600 tokens.
- `rules/README.md` rewritten: the conditional table is the index; resident
  files are listed, not described. `on-start.sh` no longer cats it.
- `tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py` caps the resident set and requires every
  resident file to be declared. `tests/test_harness_rules_injection.sh` rewritten:
  it asserted the hook did not leak bodies and passed for months while the
  runtime loaded them — an all-clear indistinguishable from "I could not look".

## Still open, ranked

1. Trim `workflow.md` (7 918 tk) and `git.md` (6 053 tk) — steps 1, 3, 4 below.
   Highest yield, needs the author's judgment on every cut.
2. Move the situation-scoped bodies that no path glob can express
   (`knowledge-hints` 2 030 tk, `edm` 1 146, `manuscript-build` 946,
   `systemd-units` 846, `pdf-finishing` 686, `prose/cutting` 473,
   `submission-events` 370) out of `rules/` into a directory the runtime does
   not auto-load, leaving pointers in the index. ~6 500 tk, mechanical, but it
   is a layout decision: it splits "doctrine" from "reference the agent fetches".
3. Step 2 below (provider-neutral core vs Claude Code idiosyncrasies) now has a
   mechanical lever: what an adapter must inject is exactly the resident set.

## Trimming pass, 2026-09-09 (second PR)

`workflow.md` 31 672 → 13 766 chars, `git.md` 24 212 → 11 988. New
`rules/claude-code.md` (4 055, resident but skippable by a non-Claude-Code
adapter) and `rules/authoring-skills.md` (conditional on `**/SKILL.md`, so
0 resident). Resident total 86 598 → 61 876 chars, 21 649 → 15 469 tokens;
`RESIDENT_BUDGET` lowered 88 000 → 63 000.

An independent editorial pass (Fable 5.1, read-only) ranked every block in
parallel and found eight contradictions. Four are fixed here:

- **Escalation asked for a memory write the same file says is denied.** "Save a
  feedback memory at each escalation" against § Worktree paths, where the
  platform gate refuses memory writes for the whole life of a worktree session.
  Now: note it at each escalation, save it at the wrap-up.
- **The memory carve-out gave two answers** (land it via PR / write it directly
  after leaving). One answer now, and the stale claim that `skills/memory/`
  instructs a direct mid-worktree write is gone — it does not.
- **The severity floor did not govern the loophole rule**, which offered a
  ticket for any gap. Folded: above the floor a ticket, below it fix or record.
- **`on-start.sh` contradicted the `/hunt` carve-out and was imperative** —
  "Use EnterWorktree as your first action" — which the harness's own rule says
  the model classifies as prompt injection. Rewritten declaratively, with the
  hunt exception named.

Two are recorded, not fixed: the local-fast-forward clause for chores (deleted
here as obsolete against the closed gate) and the tool-agnostic-language rule
being violated by the files that state it (`gh`, `/gaze`, `EnterWorktree`) —
that one is the adapter question, ticket 0800, not a defect in these files.

## Evidence evicted from the resident set, kept here

The invariant is "no rule dropped without its lesson recorded". No rule was
dropped; four incident narratives were, and this is their record.

- **Red main is a broadcast** (2026-09-06). One stale ticket-log stamp was
  corrected in two sessions at once, whose different but equally defensible
  values (`15:30Z`, `15:33Z`) conflicted at the merge gate and burned a `/gaze`
  round. Hours later a whole branch, ticket and PR for four more stale stamps
  was built and closed unmerged, a sibling having landed byte-identical
  corrections while it was in flight.
- **Three zeros are one zero measured three times** (2026-08-22). Three probes
  over ten hours counted zero Zotero structure-extraction packs and the
  conclusion drifted to a verdict on the feature. The artifact honestly recorded
  "NO POSITIVE CONTROL"; the caveat changed nothing. A fourth probe, after a
  human opened two PDFs, found two packs within a minute.
- **The division that inverted a conclusion** (2026-08-29). An upstream issue
  reported ~95 s per query over 255 703 rows; dividing gave ~390 µs per row,
  making the arithmetic look like 6% of runtime and the proposed fix pointless
  — written to the author on that basis. A real scan of that shape measures
  38 µs per row: arithmetic was 73%, the fix was worth 2,19x. One script,
  eleven minutes.
- **What set the ticket arrival rate** (2026-07-27). climate-finance filed 70
  tickets in one day against 30 closed, leaving 97 open, while its PR pipeline
  drained cleanly at 94 merged — thirteen sessions each sweeping, not a repo
  type.

## Not done — what the exit criteria still need

Step 5 (a review mechanism that catches growth, not only age) is met by
`tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py`. The remaining exit criterion is the
author's: read the trimmed `workflow.md` and accept or reverse the cuts. Also
still open: the seven situation-scoped bodies (~6 300 tk) that no path glob can
express, which need a directory the runtime does not auto-load.

## Relevant files

- `rules/workflow.md` — the file the note names; every section has grown by incident bullets
- `rules/git.md` — same shape, the longest rule body
- `rules/README.md` — index and the review-cadence policy
- `scripts/warn-stale-rules.sh` — the advisory that was meant to prevent the drift
- `tickets/0802-port-perch-through-three-adapters.erg` — the adapter work that needs a provider-neutral core

## Actions

1. Read `rules/workflow.md` whole and rank each bullet: keep, condense, move to a memory or a ticket, delete.
2. Separate the Claude Code specifics (tool names, EnterWorktree, hook mechanics) from the provider-neutral core so the Pi and Codex adapters can load the core alone.
3. Resolve the contradictions found in step 1 (the note names "do not delegate simple tasks" against the delegation doctrine).
4. Repeat the pass on `rules/git.md`; survey the other rule bodies for the same shape.
5. Make the review mechanism catch growth, not only age: for instance warn when a body has gained more than N lines since its stamp, or require the stamp to be bumped by a pass that names what it removed.

## Verification

- [ ] `rules/workflow.md` shorter than before, with a closing point that is not cosmetic
- [ ] No bullet in `rules/workflow.md` contradicts another rule file
- [ ] Claude Code idiosyncrasies live in a clearly separated file or section
- [ ] The review mechanism fires on a fixture that grew without a review pass

## Invariants

- `make check` passes; the agnostic guards on `rules/` still pass
- No rule is dropped without its lesson recorded in memory or in a ticket

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Steps 1 to 5 done and verified as above
- [ ] The author has read the trimmed `workflow.md` and accepted the cuts
